1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,026
Hi, and welcome to the CRM Scoring podcast.

2
00:00:04,626 --> 00:00:09,728
Every sales team has the same problem: too many leads and not enough hours.

3
00:00:09,728 --> 00:00:13,085
So someone has to decide who gets the first call.

4
00:00:13,085 --> 00:00:19,999
In most teams, that decision is made by gut feeling — or by whoever spoke loudest in the last meeting.

5
00:00:20,599 --> 00:00:23,138
Today I want to show you a simpler way.

6
00:00:23,138 --> 00:00:24,788
It's called lead scoring.

7
00:00:25,388 --> 00:00:31,807
Lead scoring means giving every lead a number, so you can sort your list from the most promising to the least.

8
00:00:31,807 --> 00:00:33,657
Two things go into that number.

9
00:00:34,257 --> 00:00:35,331
The first is fit.

10
00:00:35,331 --> 00:00:38,253
Does this company look like your best customers?

11
00:00:38,253 --> 00:00:45,111
Think about the industry, the size of the company, the country, and the job title of the person you're talking to.

12
00:00:45,111 --> 00:00:50,240
A marketing director at a two-hundred-person software company might be a perfect fit.

13
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:53,282
A student downloading a free guide probably isn't.

14
00:00:53,882 --> 00:00:55,481
The second is intent.

15
00:00:55,481 --> 00:00:58,461
Is this lead showing interest right now?

16
00:00:58,461 --> 00:01:04,857
Did they visit your pricing page, open your emails, ask for a demo, reply to a message?

17
00:01:05,457 --> 00:01:07,229
Fit tells you who could buy.

18
00:01:07,229 --> 00:01:09,489
Intent tells you who might buy soon.

19
00:01:10,089 --> 00:01:12,971
Put the two together and you get three groups.

20
00:01:13,571 --> 00:01:16,623
Hot leads fit well and are active now.

21
00:01:16,623 --> 00:01:17,954
Call them today.

22
00:01:18,554 --> 00:01:23,406
Warm leads fit, but they're quiet — or they're active, but not a perfect fit.

23
00:01:23,406 --> 00:01:27,885
Keep them warm with useful emails, and check back in a couple of weeks.

24
00:01:28,485 --> 00:01:31,135
Cold leads don't fit and aren't active.

25
00:01:31,135 --> 00:01:33,587
Don't spend your best hours on them.

26
00:01:34,187 --> 00:01:36,757
Here's a simple way to start tomorrow.

27
00:01:36,757 --> 00:01:38,207
Take your CRM export.

28
00:01:38,207 --> 00:01:42,821
Give each lead up to fifty points for fit and up to fifty for intent.

29
00:01:43,421 --> 00:01:51,409
For fit: ten points for the right industry, ten for the right company size, ten for a decision-maker job title.

30
00:01:52,009 --> 00:01:59,484
For intent: twenty points for a visit to your pricing page, twenty for a demo request, ten for a reply.

31
00:02:00,084 --> 00:02:02,663
Anything over seventy is hot.

32
00:02:02,663 --> 00:02:04,899
Forty to seventy is warm.

33
00:02:04,899 --> 00:02:06,704
Below forty is cold.

34
00:02:07,304 --> 00:02:11,186
It won't be perfect — but it will already be better than guessing.

35
00:02:11,786 --> 00:02:14,104
Now, three mistakes to avoid.

36
00:02:14,704 --> 00:02:16,636
One: scoring only on fit.

37
00:02:16,636 --> 00:02:22,061
A perfect company that hasn't touched your website in a year is not hot.

38
00:02:22,661 --> 00:02:24,869
Two: never updating the scores.

39
00:02:24,869 --> 00:02:28,939
A lead's score should change when their behaviour changes.

40
00:02:29,539 --> 00:02:31,158
Three: hiding the reason.

41
00:02:31,158 --> 00:02:38,132
If a salesperson can't see why a lead is hot, they won't trust the number — and they'll go back to gut feeling.

42
00:02:38,732 --> 00:02:42,421
That last point is exactly why we built CRM Scoring.

43
00:02:42,421 --> 00:02:52,515
You upload your CRM export, and every lead gets Hot, Warm or Cold, together with a short explanation in plain words: why this lead, and why now.

44
00:02:52,515 --> 00:02:59,198
It can send the results back to Salesforce, and send a Slack alert the moment a lead turns hot.

45
00:02:59,798 --> 00:03:02,148
You can try it free at crmscoring.com.

46
00:03:02,148 --> 00:03:08,054
There's also a free lead scoring template on the site, so you can start with a spreadsheet today.

47
00:03:08,654 --> 00:03:10,383
That's it for this episode.

48
00:03:10,383 --> 00:03:15,200
Next time, we'll talk about the signals that tell you a lead is ready to buy.

49
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,814
Thanks for listening — and good luck with your calls.
